$x=-0.6,2.0-1.28-7=-5.28<0$ and finally $x=-0.5,2.0-1.0-7=-6.0<0$ and at $x=(-0.4, 0.1)$ we get a positive value, thus there are two roots, a positive one in the interval from $(-0.5, -0.4)$ and a negative one somewhere between the interval $(-1.1, -0.79)$. Now we have shown that there are definitely two real roots. Let us differentiate the polynomial: \begin{align*}
\frac{d}{d x}(2x^{5}+8x-7) &= 10x^{4}+8 .
\end{align*}
Since $x \in \mathbb{R}$ and all terms are of even degree, $\forall x \in \mathbb{R},\ 10x^{4} + 8 > 0$, from this we obtain that the function $2x^{5}+8x-7$ has no local extrema. The answer is (G).
